{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>To elohim.</b> To a foreign god; had it been vocalized לֵאלֹהִים, it would have needed to specify and write 'others.' Now that it says לָאֱלֹהִים, it does not need to specify 'others' — for every lamed and bet serving at the beginning of a word, if its vowel is a ḥataf, such as לְמֶלֶךְ, לְמִדְבָּר, לְעִיר, one must specify: to which king, to which desert, to which city; and likewise לִמְלָכִים and לִרְגָלִים one must specify to which; and if he does not specify, all kings are implied — so too לֵאלֹהִים, all elohim are implied, even sacred [objects]; but when it is vocalized with a patach, like לַמֶּלֶךְ, לַמִּדְבָּר, לָעִיר, it becomes known about which king it is speaking, and likewise לָעִיר — it becomes known about which city it is speaking; and so לָאֱלֹהִים — to those about whom you were warned elsewhere; its parallel is אֵין כָּמוֹךָ בָּאֱלֹהִים — because it did not specify, it required a patach vocalization." "<b>Shall be banned.</b> Put to death. Why is יָחֳרָם stated — was not death already stated for it elsewhere, ״וְהוֹצֵאתָ אֶת הָאִישׁ הַהוּא אוֹ אֶת הָאִשָּׁה הַהִוא וְגוֹ״ (Deuteronomy 17)? Rather, since it did not specify for which mode of worship one is liable to death — so that you would not say all forms of worship incur death — it comes and specifies for you here 'זֹבֵחַ' to elohim, to tell you: just as slaughter is a form of worship performed inwardly toward heaven, so too I extend to include one who offers incense and one who pours a libation, which are forms of worship performed inwardly, and they are liable for all foreign worship, whether its customary mode of worship is through that, or whether its customary mode of worship is not through that. But other forms of worship, such as bowing, prostrating, embracing, and kissing, are not [punished by] death."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:01.327601041Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}